#X obj 674 8 data.dict;
#X obj 230 78 cnv 1 535 461 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -237178 -1 0;
#X text 240 83 A dictionary container: stores key-value pairs \, where
each key is unique and maps to exactly one value. Values can be numbers
\, symbols or lists \, and keys can be used to quickly look up \, add
\, update or remove entries. Unlike [data.bimap] \, a dictionary is
one-directional: it maps keys to values \, but not values to keys.;
#X text 240 184 Syntax: [key1: value key2: "with space"]. Nested dictionaries
are supported: [a: [A:1 B:2]].;
#X text 240 224 Typical use cases:;
#X text 250 249 • Preset storage — saving named parameters such as
"cutoff" \, "resonance" or "reverb_mix" together with their values
\, then loading and modifying them by name.;
#X text 250 304 • Configuration files — reading and writing JSON files
where each parameter has a name \, using the dictionary as an in-memory
representation of the patch state.;
#X text 250 359 • Named event handlers — mapping event names to lists
of actions \, so that incoming messages can be dispatched by their
name in one lookup.;
#X text 250 414 • Structured data — grouping related values under one
name \, for example storing all parameters of a synthesizer under "synth1"
as a nested dictionary.;
#X text 250 469 • Parameter routing — converting symbolic parameter
names (received from a GUI or a text file) into the actual values expected
by downstream objects.;
